PostgreSQL - String Functions
1. What are String Functions?-->String functions are functions used to work with text/string data.
For example, we can use them to:
Change uppercase/lowercase
Combine text
Find the length of text
Remove extra spaces
Extract part of a string
Replace text


#Common String Functions
I. CONCAT()-->Combines multiple strings.
SELECT CONCAT('Hello', ' ', 'World');
Hello World
select concat(fname, lanme) from employees;
select emp_id, concat(fname,'', lname) as fullnsme, dept from employees;
emp_id | fullname     | dept
     1 | shashikhadka | IT 
select emp_id, concat('', fname, lname, salary) as fullname, dept from employess;
emp_id |     fullname       | dept
    1  | raj sharma 500000  | IT 


II. SUBSTRING()--->Extracts a part of a string.
SELECT SUBSTRING('PostgreSQL'  1,4);
result: Post

III. REPLACE()-->Replaces part of a string with another value.
syn:REPLACE(string, old_text, new_text)
SELECT REPLACE('I like python, like, love):
result:i love python

select replace (dept,'IT','TECH') from employees;


IV. REVERSE()--> reserse the word
select reverse (fname) from employess;
result:ihsahs

v. LENGTH()--> 
select LENGTH('hello');
result: 5
select length(fname) from employees;

exercise:
select * from employees where length(fname)>5;

vi. upper ()--> Converts text to uppercase.
SELECT UPPER('postgresql');

vii. LOWER()-->Converts text to lowercase.
SELECT LOWER('POSTGRESQL');

viii. TRIM()-->Removes spaces from the beginning and end of a string.
SELECT TRIM('   Hello   ');
RESULT: Hello

